6.5 Combining It All
We now have enough tools to answer a realistic question: for each variety, among fields that received at least 100 kg/ha of nitrogen, what was the mean yield in tonnes per hectare, and how many fields were observed? Show the highest-yielding variety first.


variety_summary_100 &lt;- trial_data |&gt;
  filter(fertilizer_kg_ha &gt;= 100) |&gt;
  mutate(yield_t_ha = yield_bu_acre * 0.0560) |&gt;
  group_by(variety) |&gt;
  summarise(mean_yield_t_ha = mean(yield_t_ha, na.rm = TRUE),
            n_fields = n()) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(mean_yield_t_ha))
variety_summary_100

&gt; variety_summary_100 &lt;- trial_data |&gt;
+   filter(fertilizer_kg_ha &gt;= 100) |&gt;
+   mutate(yield_t_ha = yield_bu_acre * 0.0560) |&gt;
+   group_by(variety) |&gt;
+   summarise(mean_yield_t_ha = mean(yield_t_ha, na.rm = TRUE),
+             n_fields = n()) |&gt;
+   arrange(desc(mean_yield_t_ha))
&gt; variety_summary_100
# A tibble: 3 × 3
  variety    mean_yield_t_ha n_fields
  &lt;chr&gt;                &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;
1 InVigor               3.10       38
2 Clearfield            3.06       34
3 DEKALB                2.98       34



Do not read it as one giant command; read it one step at a time:

Filter to the fields that received at least 100 kg/ha.
Create the variable the question needs: metric yield.
Group the observations by variety.
Summarise each group down to one row: its mean yield and its number of fields.
Sort from highest mean yield to lowest.

Each line does one simple thing, and that is the reason for pipes: a good pipeline makes the logic of an analysis visible. Saving the table is one more line: write_csv(variety_summary_100, "output/variety_summary_100.csv").

15.3 A Useful Prompt Structure
A graphing request should state four things:

the data frame and relevant columns;
the rows or groups to include;
the calculation required before plotting;
the chart form and the specific design change needed.

For example:

The data frame d has columns Crop, Year, Risk_Zone, Acres and Yield. Keep recorded rows for Wheat - Hard Red Spring, Canola/Rapeseed, Barley and Oats. Calculate unweighted mean Yield by Year and Crop, then plot one line per crop. Put each crop name at the right end of its line and remove the legend. Show the summary table before the plot code.

Asking for the summary table is useful. It separates the calculation from the drawing and gives you something numerical to inspect. For a workbook prompt, paste the header and several made-up rows and state which fields are categories—Risk_Zone is an identifier, not a quantity.
